PUBLIC RESPONSE: {26 and 34}

The poem is a double sestina whose end-word sequence (mountains/valleys/forests/music/morning/evening and their prescribed rotations) is otherwise intact; therefore the swapped lines must terminate with the same end-word so that the formal pattern is preserved. Lines 26 and 34 both end with “morning.”  

Restoring them yields two locally coherent stanzas that also restore the larger rhetorical pattern of anaphora (“Long since …”) that governs the surrounding lines. All other candidate pairs either break the end-word sequence or produce grammatical or rhetorical nonsense. Hence the only transposition consistent with both the strict sestina form and the poem’s internal style is 26 and 34.}

