PUBLIC RESPONSE: {25 and 74}

The double sestina's end-word rotation (standard 6-1-5-2-4-3 permutation of mountains/valleys/forests/music/morning/evening) is already satisfied everywhere except where two identically terminating lines have exchanged places. Lines 25 and 74 are the sole such pair; both end on the required terminal ("music").  

Swapping them simultaneously repairs the sole grammatical rupture in the text ("These forests eke... Hath" becomes the singular-subject construction "Long since, alas, my deadly swannish music / Hath made itself...") and restores the canonical three-line envoi ("These mountains... valleys; / These forests eke... music; / Our morning hymn... evening"). No other candidate pair satisfies both the formal skeleton and the internal syntax simultaneously.

